Structure of a project
======================

The QMEAN project archive is composed of following files:


├── project.json
    Internal parameters of the project.

├── README
    This file

├── MODELNAME_MODELID

    ├── MODELID_original.pdb
        Originally uploaded structure in pdb format.

    ├── MODELID_raw.pdb
        Raw model used for calculations - invalid residues, ligands were
        stripped off. Local scores are set as B-factors.

    ├── MODELID_processed.pdb
        Model processed for the calculation and visualization purposes. In
        particular, this model is renumbered according to the SEQRES (ATOMSEQ
        if SEQRES is not given as an input). Local scores are set as B-factors.

    ├── model.png
        2D thumbnail colored by predicted local quality.

    ├── index.html
        Model quality report that can be displayed in your webbrowser.

    ├── score_file.json
        Model specific scores stored as dictionary in json format. No matter 
        what mode you're running (QMEAN [1] /QMEANDisCo [2] /QMEANBrane [3]), 
        you have two keys: "local_scores" and "global_scores". While the data 
        in "global_scores" calculates values according [1], the "local_scores"
        are mode dependent.

        For "global_scores" you get another dictionary with keys:

            * "acc_agreement_norm_score"
            * "acc_agreement_z_score"
            * "avg_local_score" (mode dependent)
            * "avg_local_score_error" (only available in QMEANDisCo mode)
            * "cbeta_norm_score"
            * "cbeta_z_score"
            * "interaction_norm_score"
            * "interaction_z_score"
            * "packing_norm_score"
            * "packing_z_score"
            * "qmean4_norm_score"
            * "qmean4_z_score"
            * "qmean6_norm_score"
            * "qmean6_z_score"
            * "ss_agreement_norm_score"
            * "ss_agreement_z_score"
            * "torsion_norm_score"
            * "torsion_z_score"

        For "local_scores" you get another dictionary with chain names as keys
        and lists with local scores as value. The local score lists have the
        same length as the according SEQRES (ATOMSEQ if SEQRES is not given as 
        an input). The location of a residue in SEQRES determines the location
        of its local score in the score list.

    ├── mem_info.json
        Membrane specific information stored as dict in json format, only 
        available in QMEANBrane mode. The "in_membrane" entry is another
        dictionary with chain names as keys and lists of bool as value.
        The lists have the same length as the according SEQRES 
        (ATOMSEQ if SEQRES is not given as an input). The location of a 
        residue in SEQRES determines the location in the list. If an entry is
        True, it is predicted to be within two planes defining the membrane. 
        If a residue in the SEQRES is not covered by the model, its value is 
        set to None.
        The location of the membrane is defined by:

            * "membrane_axis": the x,y,z values of the membrane normal
            * "membrane_energy": Result of the implicit solvation model 
                                 that has been minimized in order to locate
                                 the membrane
            * "membrane_pos": The center of the membrane is defined as:
                              membrane_pos * membrane_axis
            * "width": The width of the membrane in A

    ├── energy_gap.png
        Comparison of "membrane_energy" available in mem_info.json with
        the outcome from a non-redundant set of soluble and membrane
        proteins. Only available in QMEANBrane mode.
        
MODELNAME - The original name of the file uploaded.
MODELID   - ID of the model assigned by QMEAN web server.
CHAINNAME - Name of the chain eg. A. If for any reason there is no chain name
            provided in the original file, the name will be set up
            automatically.


For more information refer to https://swissmodel.expasy.org/qmean/help.


References
==========

Reference for the QMEAN scoring function:
[1] Benkert, P., Biasini, M., Schwede, T. Toward the estimation of the absolute
quality of individual protein structure models. Bioinformatics 27, 343-350
(2011).

Reference for the QMEANDisCo scoring function:
[2] Studer, G., Rempfer, C., Waterhouse, A.M., Gumienny, G., Haas, J., 
Schwede, T. QMEANDisCo - distance constraints applied on model quality 
estimation. Bioinformatics 36, 1765-1771 (2020).

Reference for the QMEANBrane scoring function:
[3] Studer, G., Biasini, M., Schwede, T. Assessing the local structural quality
of transmembrane protein models using statistical potentials (QMEANBrane).
Bioinformatics 30, i505-11 (2014).


Thank you for using QMEAN!
